{%- comment -%}
  AJ Daily Deal — the featured deal of the day.

  Resolves the active deal itself rather than making someone pick one every
  morning: it walks the `daily_deal` metaobject entries and takes the one whose
  start/end window contains "now". Queue a week of deals in advance and they
  rotate on their own.

  Requires a `daily_deal` metaobject definition with these fields:

    product             product_reference  the deal item
    starts_at           date_time          when it goes live
    ends_at             date_time          when it stops — drives the clock
    units_allocated     number_integer     units committed; NOT a cap — see aj-deal-claimed
    starting_inventory  number_integer     stock on hand at open; see below
    headline            single_line_text   optional eyebrow, e.g. "Today only"

  `starting_inventory` is what makes the sold count work on dropshipped
  products. The supplier holds the stock and it dwarfs any allocation we make,
  so the count measures the drop from this snapshot rather than assuming we
  started with our allocation on the shelf.

  Renders nothing on the storefront when no deal is active, so an empty queue
  degrades to an absent section rather than a broken one. In the theme editor it
  falls back to the first entry so there is something to design against.

  NOTE ON CACHING: the active-deal lookup happens server-side at render time. At
  a deal boundary a visitor may briefly see the outgoing deal from cache; the
  countdown is client-side against an absolute timestamp, so it stays correct
  regardless and hits zero on schedule.
{%- endcomment -%}

<script src="{{ 'aj-daily-deal.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
<script src="{{ 'aj-variant-select.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>

{%- liquid
  assign now = 'now' | date: '%s' | plus: 0
  assign deal = null

  for entry in shop.metaobjects.daily_deal.values
    assign starts = entry.starts_at.value | date: '%s' | plus: 0
    assign ends = entry.ends_at.value | date: '%s' | plus: 0
    if starts <= now and ends > now
      assign deal = entry
      break
    endif
  endfor

  comment
    Editor-only fallback. Never applies on the live storefront — a merchant
    designing the section needs to see it, a customer must not see an expired
    or unstarted deal.
  endcomment
  if deal == null and request.design_mode
    assign deal = shop.metaobjects.daily_deal.values.first
  endif

  assign product = deal.product.value
  assign variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant

  comment
    The hero follows the picker, and the picker starts on `variant`. Rendering
    product.featured_image instead left the page opening on one color while the
    dropdown named another — on this catalogue the deal product is often a
    single form in nine glazes, so the mismatch is the whole decision.

    NOT EVERY VARIANT HAS ITS OWN PHOTO. Suppliers shoot what they shoot: of the
    nine colors on the current deal, four have no image and two share one. So
    every lookup falls back to the product image rather than blanking, here and
    in the script.
  endcomment
  assign hero_image = variant.image | default: product.featured_image

  assign on_sale = false
  if variant.compare_at_price > variant.price
    assign on_sale = true
    assign savings = variant.compare_at_price | minus: variant.price
    assign savings_percent = savings | times: 100 | divided_by: variant.compare_at_price
  endif
-%}

{%- if product != blank -%}
  <div
    class="aj-deal color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }} section spacing-style"
    style="{% render 'spacing-style', settings: section.settings %}"
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      Marks how far the picker may reach to find the hero image. The picker sits
      in the info column and the image is in the media column, so it cannot just
      look inside its own row the way the cart upsell does.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="aj-deal__inner page-width" data-aj-variant-scope>
      {%- comment -%}
        The hero image loads EAGER, unlike everything else on the page. It is
        the largest element above the fold and the thing the whole homepage is
        about — lazy-loading it left a blank half-screen beside the price on
        first paint, and made the deal photo the page's LCP element arriving
        last. Every other image here stays lazy.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        A GALLERY, BECAUSE LEAVING THE PAGE COSTS THE COUNTDOWN.

        The Yoink is a one-shot pitch: there is no second visit to evaluate the
        product, and the only way to see a second photo was to click through to
        the product page — away from the timer and the buy button, onto a page
        where the urgency is gone. This keeps the research on the deal.

        DOTS, NOT THUMBNAILS. This card already sits beside a tall content
        column; a thumbnail strip would eat the vertical space that keeps the
        two halves balanced. Dots say "there is more" without competing with
        the price.

        THE FIRST SLIDE KEEPS `data-aj-variant-image`. aj-variant-select.js
        swaps the hero when a variant changes by querying that marker, so it has
        to stay on slide one and stay first in the DOM. The gallery scrolls back
        to it on a variant change rather than leaving someone looking at the
        old color's third photo.

        Everything below degrades without JavaScript: the track is a scroll
        container, so it swipes and scrolls natively and the controls are only
        an enhancement.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign gallery = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'image' -%}
      <div
        class="aj-deal__media"
        data-aj-gallery
        data-count="{{ gallery.size }}"
      >
        <div class="aj-deal__track" data-aj-gallery-track>
          {%- if hero_image -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              The marker sits on the anchor, not the img: `image_tag` takes named
              arguments, and Liquid cannot parse a hyphenated one like
              `data-aj-variant-image:`.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <a class="aj-deal__slide" href="{{ product.url }}" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" data-aj-variant-image>
              {{
                hero_image
                | image_url: width: 1200
                | image_tag:
                  loading: 'eager',
                  fetchpriority: 'high',
                  sizes: '(min-width: 750px) 50vw, 100vw',
                  widths: '400, 600, 800, 1000, 1200',
                  class: 'aj-deal__image',
                  alt: hero_image.alt
              }}
            </a>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            The rest. `hero_image` is skipped by id so a variant image that is
            also in product.media does not appear twice. These stay lazy — only
            the hero is the LCP element.

            COMPARED AGAINST `preview_image`, NOT THE MEDIA ITSELF, and that is
            the whole reason this works at all. `hero_image` is an IMAGE —
            `variant.image`, falling back to `product.featured_image` — while
            `gallery` holds MEDIA. The two carry ids from different spaces, so
            `media.id == hero_image.id` is never true and the skip never fired:
            every deal rendered its hero twice and the dot count was one short.
            It hid for months because the duplicate landed mid-carousel; the
            moment a variant image was also first in media order the two
            identical slides became adjacent. `media.preview_image` is the
            Image behind the Media, so this compares like with like.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- for media in gallery -%}
            {%- if hero_image and media.preview_image.id == hero_image.id -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
            <a class="aj-deal__slide" href="{{ product.url }}" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
              {{
                media
                | image_url: width: 1200
                | image_tag:
                  loading: 'lazy',
                  sizes: '(min-width: 750px) 50vw, 100vw',
                  widths: '400, 600, 800, 1000, 1200',
                  class: 'aj-deal__image',
                  alt: media.alt
              }}
            </a>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          Rendered always, hidden by CSS when there is nothing to page through —
          `[data-count="1"]` and `[data-count="0"]`. Building them conditionally
          in Liquid would mean counting the deduped slides twice.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <button class="aj-deal__nav aj-deal__nav--prev" type="button" data-aj-gallery-prev aria-label="Previous image">
          <span aria-hidden="true">&#8249;</span>
        </button>
        <button class="aj-deal__nav aj-deal__nav--next" type="button" data-aj-gallery-next aria-label="Next image">
          <span aria-hidden="true">&#8250;</span>
        </button>
        <div class="aj-deal__dots" data-aj-gallery-dots role="tablist" aria-label="Product images"></div>

        {%- if on_sale -%}
          <span class="aj-deal__badge">Save {{ savings_percent }}%</span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      <div class="aj-deal__content">
        {%- comment -%}
          AN EYEBROW THAT REPEATS THE TITLE IS NOT AN EYEBROW. The app writes the
          product's own name into the entry's `headline` field when a deal is
          queued, so the purple label rendered the product name and the <h2>
          repeated it verbatim underneath — the same words twice in two faces.
          Every queued entry carries the same value, so this is guarded here
          rather than by editing one metaobject: the label falls back to the
          section default whenever the headline is just the title again.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign eyebrow = deal.headline.value | default: section.settings.default_headline -%}
        {%- if eyebrow == product.title -%}
          {%- assign eyebrow = section.settings.default_headline -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if eyebrow != blank -%}
          <p class="aj-deal__eyebrow">{{ eyebrow | escape }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}

        <h2 class="aj-deal__title">
          <a href="{{ product.url }}">{{ product.title | escape }}</a>
        </h2>

        <div class="aj-deal__pricing">
          <span class="aj-deal__price" data-aj-upsell-price>{{ variant.price | money }}</span>
          {%- if on_sale -%}
            <s class="aj-deal__compare">{{ variant.compare_at_price | money }}</s>
            <span class="aj-deal__savings">You save {{ savings | money }}</span>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          SHIPS FREE, ON THE ONE PAGE THAT DID NOT SAY IT.

          The pill already exists and already renders on the Bargain Bin, Past
          Deals and Shop by Price. The daily deal — the page the whole store
          points at, and the one carrying paid traffic — was the only place
          missing it. Today's Yoink ships free and said nothing.

          Shipping is the last number a shopper sees and the one most likely to
          kill a cheap deal. When it is zero that is the strongest thing on the
          page after the price, and it was being withheld by accident.

          THE EXACT TEST THE OTHER SECTIONS USE, deliberately identical: a real
          `draftOrderCalculate` quote of zero, never the vendor's general shape —
          a supplier who ships most things free can still charge on a heavy one.
          Blank means unmeasured and renders nothing. Silence is not "free".
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          COERCED WITH `| plus: 0`, which is the whole reason the first attempt
          rendered nothing. `ship.cost` is a `number_decimal`, and through a
          metaobject reference its value arrives as a STRING — "0.0" == 0 is
          false in Liquid, so the test silently failed on a product that does
          ship free. The bin's copy of this works because it reads the product
          from a collection loop, where the value is already numeric.

          The blank check stays on the METAFIELD, not the coerced number:
          `blank | plus: 0` is 0, so testing the coerced value would report every
          unmeasured product as shipping free — the exact lie this guard exists
          to prevent.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign ship_mf = product.metafields.ship.cost -%}
        {%- if ship_mf != blank -%}
          {%- assign deal_ship = ship_mf.value | plus: 0 -%}
          {%- if deal_ship == 0 -%}
            <p class="aj-card-free aj-deal__ship">Ships free</p>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if section.settings.show_countdown and deal.ends_at.value != blank -%}
          {% render 'aj-deal-countdown',
            ends_at: deal.ends_at.value,
            reload_on_expire: section.settings.reload_on_expire,
            heading: section.settings.countdown_heading
          %}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if section.settings.show_claimed -%}
          {% render 'aj-deal-claimed',
            product: product,
            units_allocated: deal.units_allocated.value,
            starting_inventory: deal.starting_inventory.value,
            min_sold: settings.sold_min_count,
            label: settings.sold_label
          %}
        {%- endif -%}

        <div class="aj-deal__cta" data-aj-upsell-row>
          {%- comment -%}
            Add to cart HERE, options and all. Sending someone to the product
            page to pick a color loses most of them, and this is the one item on
            the site we most want bought — so the same picker the cart upsell
            uses appears inline instead.

            Built on Horizon's product-form-component, which posts to the cart,
            asks for the cart sections back and morphs them in. With
            auto_open_cart_drawer on, the drawer opens by itself.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if variant.available -%}
            {%- assign deal_form_id = 'AjDeal-' | append: product.id -%}

            {%- unless product.variants.size == 1 -%}
              <aj-variant-select class="aj-deal__variants">
                <label class="visually-hidden" for="{{ deal_form_id }}-variant">
                  Option for {{ product.title | escape }}
                </label>
                <select id="{{ deal_form_id }}-variant" class="aj-deal__select">
                  {%- for v in product.variants -%}
                    {%- assign v_image = v.image | default: product.featured_image -%}
                    <option
                      value="{{ v.id }}"
                      data-price="{{ v.price | money }}"
                      data-available="{{ v.available }}"
                      {%- if v_image %}
                        data-image-src="{{ v_image | image_url: width: 1200 }}"
                        data-image-srcset="{{ v_image | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w, {{ v_image | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w, {{ v_image | image_url: width: 1200 }} 1200w"
                        data-image-alt="{{ v_image.alt | default: product.title | escape }}"
                      {%- endif %}
                      {% if v.id == variant.id %}selected{% endif %}
                      {% unless v.available %}disabled{% endunless %}
                    >
                      {{ v.title | escape }}
                      {%- unless v.available %} — sold out{% endunless -%}
                    </option>
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </select>
              </aj-variant-select>
            {%- endunless -%}

            <product-form-component
              data-section-id="{{ section.id }}"
              data-product-id="{{ product.id }}"
              on:submit="/handleSubmit"
              class="aj-deal__form-component"
            >
              <div
                class="visually-hidden"
                aria-live="assertive"
                role="status"
                aria-atomic="true"
                ref="liveRegion"
              ></div>
              {%- form 'product', product, id: deal_form_id, novalidate: 'novalidate', data-type: 'add-to-cart-form' -%}
                <input type="hidden" name="id" ref="variantId" value="{{ variant.id }}">
                <input type="hidden" name="quantity" value="1">
                <button type="submit" class="button aj-deal__button">
                  {{ 'actions.add_to_cart' | t | default: 'Add to cart' }}
                </button>
              {%- endform -%}
            </product-form-component>

            <a href="{{ product.url }}" class="aj-deal__details">See full details</a>
          {%- else -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              A SOLD-OUT YOINK IS NOT A BROKEN PAGE, and until now it read like
              one: a bare "Sold out" under a countdown still ticking, on a
              product the homepage hero and every share still point at. The deal
              deliberately stays visible when it sells out — hiding it would 404
              the announcement bar, the Meta post and every link anyone shared —
              so the page has to say something worth reading for the rest of the
              window.

              This is the best available outcome from the worst case. Someone
              arriving at a sold-out deal has already shown they wanted it, so
              peak disappointment is also peak intent for "tell me about the
              next one" — which is exactly what the block below already offers.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <p class="aj-deal__soldout">
              <strong>Gone.</strong> Today's Yoink sold out.
            </p>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          BELOW the add-to-cart, deliberately. Its whole job is to catch someone
          who is not buying today, and between the price and the button it would
          interrupt the highest-intent moment on the site with a reason to leave.
          Here it is only reached by someone who scrolled past the button.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if settings.tomorrow_enabled -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The heading changes when the deal is gone. "Today's Yoink not your
            thing?" is addressed to someone who chose not to buy; to someone who
            could not, it reads as a taunt.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign peek_heading = settings.tomorrow_heading
            unless variant.available
              assign peek_heading = settings.soldout_heading | default: "Missed it? Here's what's next."
            endunless
          -%}
          {% render 'aj-deal-tomorrow',
            heading: peek_heading,
            show_signup: settings.tomorrow_signup
          %}
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
{%- elsif request.design_mode -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Editor-only setup hint. Customers never see this branch.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="aj-deal aj-deal--empty page-width">
    <p>
      <strong>No daily deal is set up yet.</strong>
      Create a <code>daily_deal</code> metaobject entry with a product,
      <code>starts_at</code>, <code>ends_at</code>, and
      <code>units_allocated</code>, and it will appear here automatically.
    </p>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

<script src="{{ 'aj-deal-gallery.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
{{ 'aj-ships-free.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{% stylesheet %}
  /*
    NOT 50/50, AND NOT CENTRED. Both were wrong for what this section is.

    Equal columns gave the photograph and the offer identical weight, so the
    page read as a product listing rather than as today's event. The image is
    the seduction and the offer is the decision; they are not the same job and
    should not occupy the same footprint.

    `align-items: center` was the worse half. Against a tall product image the
    whole offer floated in mid-air with unequal space above and below, anchored
    to nothing. Starting both columns at the top lets the eyebrow, title, price,
    clock and button read as one descending stack, which is the order someone
    actually makes the decision in.
  */
  .aj-deal__inner {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--padding-lg, 2rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .aj-deal__inner {
      grid-template-columns: 1.15fr 0.85fr;
      gap: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
    }
  }

  /*
    The offer gets a panel so it reads as a thing rather than as loose text
    beside a picture. Tinted from the brand cyan and mixed into whatever ground
    the section's color scheme is using, so it stays a tint rather than
    assuming white. Ink on this is comfortably past 4.5:1 — the cyan is at 12%,
    nowhere near the 1.66:1 the pure color would give.
  */
  .aj-deal__content {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--aj-brand-cyan, #5cd8f8) 12%, rgb(var(--color-background-rgb, 255 255 255)));
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--aj-brand-cyan, #5cd8f8) 38%, rgb(var(--color-background-rgb, 255 255 255)));
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.9rem);
  }

  /* The image should fill its taller column rather than leaving a gap under a
     panel that is now shorter than it. */
  /*
    A FIXED BOX, because the product decides the shape otherwise.

    `height: auto` let the image take whatever aspect ratio the supplier shot
    at. A square-ish product looked fine; the first tall one — a spray bottle —
    rendered about 1100px high, towered over the whole content column and left
    a screen of dead white beside it. The deal card is the most important thing
    on the site and its height was being set by whatever the supplier
    photographed.

    Square, with `object-fit: contain`, so every Yoink occupies the same space
    whatever its shape: a tall bottle letterboxes left and right, a wide product
    letterboxes top and bottom, and the layout never moves. capped so it cannot
    outgrow the content beside it on a large screen.
  */
  .aj-deal__media {
    position: relative;
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* The scroll container. Native swipe on touch, snap so a slide never rests
     half-shown, and no scrollbar because the dots already say there is more. */
  .aj-deal__track {
    display: flex;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
    scrollbar-width: none;
    -ms-overflow-style: none;
    /* Momentum scrolling on iOS, and it stops a horizontal swipe from being
       hijacked into page navigation. */
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  }

  .aj-deal__track::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }

  .aj-deal__slide {
    flex: 0 0 100%;
    scroll-snap-align: center;
    display: block;
  }

  /* Arrows: desktop only. On touch the swipe is the affordance and the dots
     confirm it; on a mouse there is no swipe, so without these the extra
     images are effectively undiscoverable on the device where people do their
     considered browsing. */
  .aj-deal__nav {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    z-index: 2;
    display: none;
    width: 2.5rem;
    height: 2.5rem;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.92);
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1;
    cursor: pointer;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 6px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.18);
  }

  .aj-deal__nav--prev { inset-inline-start: 0.6rem; }
  .aj-deal__nav--next { inset-inline-end: 0.6rem; }
  .aj-deal__nav[disabled] { opacity: 0.35; cursor: default; }

  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .aj-deal__nav { display: grid; place-items: center; }
  }

  .aj-deal__dots {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-end: 0.75rem;
    inset-inline: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  .aj-deal__dot {
    width: 0.5rem;
    height: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 999px;
    /* Ink at low alpha rather than a grey, so it reads on both a white product
       shot and a dark one. */
    background: rgb(40 40 40 / 0.28);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background-color 0.15s ease, transform 0.15s ease;
  }

  .aj-deal__dot[aria-selected="true"] {
    background: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
    transform: scale(1.25);
  }

  /* ONE IMAGE IS NOT A GALLERY. A lone dot and two dead arrows read as broken
     controls, so at zero or one slide the whole apparatus disappears. */
  .aj-deal__media[data-count="0"] .aj-deal__dots,
  .aj-deal__media[data-count="1"] .aj-deal__dots,
  .aj-deal__media[data-count="0"] .aj-deal__nav,
  .aj-deal__media[data-count="1"] .aj-deal__nav {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .aj-deal__track { scroll-behavior: auto; }
    .aj-deal__dot { transition: none; }
  }

  .aj-deal__image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    max-height: 34rem;
    /* contain, not cover: cover would crop a tall bottle to a square and cut
       the trigger off the top. Nothing on a product photo is safe to crop. */
    object-fit: contain;
    object-position: center;
  }

  .aj-deal__badge {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: var(--padding-sm, 1rem);
    inset-inline-start: var(--padding-sm, 1rem);
    padding: 0.3em 0.7em;
    border-radius: 3px;
    /* The grab color, not the theme neutral. This badge is the single loudest
       claim on the homepage — the saving on today's Yoink — and it sat in plain
       ink because it predates the palette. Ink on yellow is 11.17:1, so it is
       both the loudest and the most readable pairing available. Falls back to
       the old neutral if the brand sheet ever fails to load. */
    background: var(--aj-price-accent, rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb)));
    color: var(--aj-price-on-accent, rgb(var(--color-background-rgb)));
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /*
    BIGGER, AND LESS FADED. At 13px and 0.75 opacity this was the quietest
    thing in a card whose entire job is to announce the day's offer — smaller
    than the body copy underneath it. It is the label that tells you what you
    are looking at, and it is set in the display face precisely so it can carry
    some size.

    Opacity to 1: Ohpixel is a pixel grid, and fading it thins already-thin
    stems rather than reading as soft. The hierarchy is held by size and case
    instead, which is what the face was drawn for.
  */
  .aj-deal__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    /*
      26px at desktop, level with the product title rather than under it.

      That is deliberate and does not flatten the hierarchy: the title is
      Archivo at 700 in near-black, this is a pixel face at 400 in purple, and
      the price is 48px above both. Rank is carried by weight, color and the
      price, so the label naming the offer can afford to be seen.

      WEIGHT IS 400 BECAUSE 600 DID NOTHING. Ohpixel ships one weight, and
      measured on the live page the rendered width is identical at 400, 600 and
      700 — the browser is not even synthesising a bold. Declaring 600 described
      an effect that was not happening, which is worse than declaring nothing.
      Presence here comes from size and tracking, which is what a pixel face
      responds to.
    */
    /*
      FLASHY, NOT THE PIXEL FACE. Ohpixel is drawn for small structural labels
      and this line is 26px announcing the day's offer — the one string on the
      page with both the size and the importance for the logo face. It is also
      our own copy, which is the standing rule for where Flashy is allowed.

      Tracking drops from 0.1em to 0.02em. Ohpixel needs air or its pixel grid
      runs together; Flashy is drawn tight and letter-spacing pulls it apart.
      The two faces want opposite treatment, so the value has to move with them.

      Weight 400 because Flashy ships one weight, exactly as Ohpixel does —
      anything heavier is the browser smearing an outline sideways, which on a
      face with this much character reads as a rendering fault.
    */
    font-family: "Flashy", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.4vw, 1.625rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /*
      Purple, measured against this card's own tinted ground rather than
      against white: 6.47:1, which clears AA for body text and is nowhere near
      the 3:1 large-text floor. (On plain white it is 6.89:1 — the tint costs
      almost nothing.)

      It is also the semantically right accent. Purple is this brand's "act"
      color — links and primary buttons — and yellow is reserved for the
      saving. An eyebrow that names the offer belongs to the first group, and
      putting it in yellow would have set it competing with the SAVE 53% badge
      a few inches to its left.
    */
    color: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
  }

  .aj-deal__title {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .aj-deal__title a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /*
    Tightened deliberately. The stack was evenly spaced, which reads as a list
    of unrelated facts; grouping the price hard against its comparison and its
    saving makes them one statement, and then leaving real air before the clock
    and the button separates deciding from acting.
  */
  .aj-deal__pricing {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    margin-block-start: 0.9rem;
    margin-block-end: 1.1rem;
  }

  /* Its own line under the price: inline with the saving it would compete with
     the number the page exists for. */
  .aj-deal__ship { margin: 0.6rem 0 0; font-size: 0.85rem; }

  .aj-deal__price {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .aj-deal__compare {
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .aj-deal__savings {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .aj-deal__cta {
    margin-block-start: var(--padding-md, 1.5rem);
  }

  .aj-deal__variants {
    display: block;
    margin-block-end: 0.6rem;
  }

  .aj-deal__select {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 22rem;
    padding: 0.55em 2em 0.55em 0.7em;
    border: 1px solid rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.35);
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: transparent;
    color: inherit;
    font: inherit;
    /* Native arrows vary wildly by platform; draw our own so the control
       matches the button underneath it. */
    appearance: none;
    background-image: linear-gradient(45deg, transparent 50%, currentColor 50%),
                      linear-gradient(135deg, currentColor 50%, transparent 50%);
    background-position: calc(100% - 1.1em) 1.05em, calc(100% - 0.8em) 1.05em;
    background-size: 0.35em 0.35em, 0.35em 0.35em;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  .aj-deal__form-component {
    display: block;
  }

  .aj-deal__button {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .aj-deal__details {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-block-start: 0.6rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .aj-deal__button {
      width: auto;
      min-width: 16rem;
    }
  }

  .aj-deal__soldout {
    margin: 0;
    font-weight: 600;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .aj-deal--empty {
    padding-block: var(--padding-xl, 3rem);
    text-align: center;
  }

{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Yoink of the Day",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Shows whichever daily_deal metaobject entry is active right now. Hidden on the storefront when nothing is scheduled."
    },
    {
      "type": "color_scheme",
      "id": "color_scheme",
      "label": "Color scheme",
      "default": "scheme-1"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "default_headline",
      "label": "Default headline",
      "default": "Yoink of the Day",
      "info": "Used when the entry has no headline of its own, or when its headline is just the product name again. Matches the label on the product page."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Countdown"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_countdown",
      "label": "Show countdown",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "countdown_heading",
      "label": "Countdown label",
      "default": "Deal ends in"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "reload_on_expire",
      "label": "Reload when the deal ends",
      "default": true,
      "info": "Refreshes 5 seconds after the clock hits zero so the next deal loads."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Claimed bar"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_claimed",
      "label": "Show % claimed",
      "default": true,
      "info": "Calculated from real inventory against the units allocated on the metaobject. Hidden automatically when the product does not track inventory."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "When it shows is set once for the whole store, in Theme settings → Deal urgency, so the homepage and the product page cannot disagree."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Padding"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-start",
      "label": "Top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 48
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-end",
      "label": "Bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 48
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Yoink of the Day"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
